INITIALS ENGRAVER

Initials Engraver — a real-world task using only today's concepts: split(), join(), strip(), replace(), upper(), indexing, looping over text, comparisons, if / else, variables, input(), and printing. No imports, no tricks.

Solve it individually, then come together as a group to compare approaches. Good luck!

A gift shop in GRA engraves names on wooden boxes. Customers send their names typed every possible wrong way: with underscores instead of spaces, with double spaces, with stray spaces at the ends. Before engraving, the shop cleans the name and works out the initials.

The program must:
- Ask for the full name as sent by the customer.
- Clean it: underscores become spaces, extra spacing collapses so words are separated by exactly one space, and no space remains at the start or end.
- Print the cleaned name in ALL CAPS on one line: ENGRAVING: ADAEZE OBI KAINEBI
- Print the initials - the first letter of every word, in order, each followed by a dot: INITIALS: A.O.K.
- Use split() and join() for the cleaning - that is the whole point of today. Build the initials by looping over the cleaned words and taking the first letter of each.

Sample run:

Customer name:  adaeze__obi   kainebi

ENGRAVING: ADAEZE OBI KAINEBI
INITIALS: A.O.K.

Edge cases to handle: a two-word name, a single word (initials is just one letter and a dot), a name typed with underscores AND double spaces together, a name with spaces at both ends, and a name that was already clean (nothing changes).
